  <section class="hero box">
    <h1>Six places where writing moves a number, with the arithmetic under each.</h1>
    <p>The scale used throughout: 300 new accounts a year, $3.3k average contract, 10,000 visits a month, an account lasting two to three years.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <p>A subscription dollar at 75–80% gross margin is close to pure margin, which makes a churned account close to pure loss — unrecovered acquisition cost plus the remaining life, around $5–20k an account. At 300 new a year and 15% leaving in the first year, that is a burn pool of roughly $225–900k annually. The six below are ways of reaching into it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="box">
    <ul class="ascent">
      <li><b>1 · The billing plumbing.</b> Of 100 cancellations, 20–40 are failed cards rather than decisions. Left alone about 6–12 return; with retry logic and card updaters, 11–28 do — 5–16 accounts held, for the least writing of anything here.</li>
      <li><b>2 · The onboarding sequence.</b> Around 44% of first-year cancellations happen inside the first 90 days, and a sequence that lands during them runs 2–3× the engagement of a batch send. Two points on 1,500 trials is 30 accounts, near $99k of new contract value, recurring every year the sequence runs.</li>
      <li><b>3 · The churn-save flow.</b> A save flow holds 10–30% of the accounts that reach it. Of 45 cancellations a year that is 5–9 accounts, worth $25–180k held. In construction the seasonal leaver takes a pause better than a discount.</li>
      <li><b>4 · The friction preview.</b> A page that shows what the first 30 days actually costs in effort moves visit-to-trial from around 3% to around 4.5% — half again as many trials on identical spend. At constant downstream that is a large number, and it is the one on this page resting most heavily on the coefficients holding.</li>
      <li><b>5 · The review cadence.</b> Under 20 reviews inside 24 months sits outside the badge layer entirely, which is a gate rather than a percentage. Reviews sourced that way convert at 33% against 25%, and each account arriving organically saves the paid chain behind it.</li>
      <li><b>6 · The tracking layer.</b> No direct figure by design. Its value is the option it opens: triggered sends run 2–5× batch, and they need events that exist and carry identity first.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="panel">
    <p>Every figure above is a direction taken from a mechanism, at a stated scale, with the intermediate metric named — engagement multiple, activation event, time to first value. None is a measurement of a past client, and none is a promise about a future one. The numbers change when a founder replaces the scale with his own.</p>
  </div>

  <section class="box">
    <p>Most of the six need the same thing first: something that reaches the trial user, and something worth saying when it does. That is what the course builds — <a class="lateral" href="/versions/v2/educational/">why the teaching comes first &rarr;</a>.</p>
  </section>

